# netdata-dispatch.env — runtime config for netdata-dispatch.sh and
# netdata-feed-push.sh.
#
# Installed to /opt/netdata-dashboardz/etc/netdata-dispatch.env by
# install.sh (mode 0640, root:netdata) only if absent — edits survive
# re-runs of install.sh. enable.sh refuses to start services while this
# file still holds the shipped defaults. Values left as `replace-me` (or
# empty) make the corresponding delivery a logged no-op, never a failure.

# Selects which script under /opt/netdata-dashboardz/bin/backends/ handles
# alert delivery. Defaults to "noop" (append to a local log) so a fresh
# install can't fire bogus alerts. Flip to "dashboardz", "telegram", or
# "fanout" (both) once the values below are filled in.
DISPATCH_BACKEND=noop

# ---- dashboardz backend ------------------------------------------------
# Pick ONE transport:
#
#   direct — the host can reach the hub (LAN, VPN, same box). Needs only
#            curl. This is the path docs/integrations.md teaches.
DASHBOARDZ_HUB_URL=replace-me        # e.g. http://hub.example.lan:8484
#
#   relay  — no route to the hub; frames travel sealed through the relay
#            (it sees ciphertext + routing metadata, never content) via
#            the `dbz-send` CLI (dashboardz repo, clients/sender — see
#            this integration's README for getting it onto a host). Leave
#            DASHBOARDZ_HUB_URL as replace-me to use this.
DASHBOARDZ_RELAY_URL=replace-me      # wss://...
DASHBOARDZ_HUB_UID=replace-me        # hub_... (printed in the hub's logs at boot)
# Path to the dbz-send binary, or a multi-word command for a vendored
# install, e.g. DBZ_SEND="node /opt/netdata-dashboardz/vendor/dbz-send/dist/cli.js"
# DBZ_SEND=dbz-send

# Both transports: the sender token, minted on the hub admin's **Senders**
# tab (shown once). One sender per host keeps the audit trail honest.
DASHBOARDZ_TOKEN=replace-me          # dbz_s_...

# Optional label prefixed to alert bodies — useful when several
# environments share one hub (e.g. "staging").
# DASHBOARDZ_HOST_LABEL=

# Opaque hub feed ids (feed_..., shown on the admin Data sources page —
# NOT the human-readable feed name). Create the feeds first: a stream feed
# for the journal, value feeds for the metrics. Make them PER HOST (a name
# convention like nd.<host>.cpu helps) — a journal feed shared between
# hosts makes every board show every host's stream. Any id left as
# replace-me skips that push, logged, not fatal.
DASHBOARDZ_FEED_JOURNAL=replace-me
DASHBOARDZ_FEED_CPU=replace-me
DASHBOARDZ_FEED_RAM=replace-me
# Optional: root-fs disk + network throughput feeds.
DASHBOARDZ_FEED_DISK=replace-me
DASHBOARDZ_FEED_NET=replace-me

# ---- telegram backend --------------------------------------------------
# Only read when DISPATCH_BACKEND=telegram (or fanout).
# Bot token: https://core.telegram.org/bots#how-do-i-create-a-bot
# Chat id: send a message to your bot, then
#   curl -s "https://api.telegram.org/bot$TOKEN/getUpdates" | jq '.result[].message.chat.id'
TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=replace-me
TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID=replace-me

# ---- fanout ------------------------------------------------------------
# Only read when DISPATCH_BACKEND=fanout. fanout.sh runs every backend
# listed here (space-separated, no .sh) in sequence, ignoring individual
# failures — this is how Telegram keeps delivering even if the hub is
# unreachable, and vice versa.
FANOUT_BACKENDS="telegram dashboardz"

# CLEAR events reach dispatch (health_alarm_notify.conf doesn't filter
# them — dashboardz's resolve path needs them to retract cards). Backends
# listed here (space-separated) are skipped for a CLEAR event specifically;
# telegram is listed by default to avoid resolve-spam in the chat.
FANOUT_SKIP_CLEAR="telegram"

# ---- critical-services watchdog ---------------------------------------
# Re-page interval while a listed service remains non-active (minutes).
# WATCHDOG_REPEAT_MINUTES=30

# ---- journal-watcher ignore list --------------------------------------
# Comma- or space-separated systemd unit names whose err-level journal
# entries should be silently dropped (bot-scan noise, upstream flaps,
# etc.). Prefer fixing the source of the log over adding here; only list
# units whose err-level output is genuinely unfixable noise.
#
# *** ALWAYS QUOTE MULTI-WORD VALUES ***
# The dispatcher sources this file with `set -a; . file; set +a`, so any
# unquoted space makes bash parse the second word as a command:
#   JOURNAL_WATCHER_IGNORE_UNITS=nginx.service redis.service   # BROKEN
#   JOURNAL_WATCHER_IGNORE_UNITS="nginx.service redis.service" # OK
# The broken form fails with `redis.service: command not found` and makes
# every dispatch call exit 127, silently dropping all alerts.
# JOURNAL_WATCHER_IGNORE_UNITS=
